you're watching the daily climate show  on sky news on today's programme  we visit the world's largest wind  turbine testing facility to find out if  the uk can  become a world leader in wind power  the beans that could save up to 4.5  billion litres of water a year in latin  america  and two technologies making air  conditioning more climate friendly  take the top prize at an international  competition but how do they work  [Music]  hello and welcome to the uk's only daily  climate news show where we track the  changes happening to our world right now  and meet those coming up with the  solutions we aim to take you to the  heart of the climate crisis  explain the data driving the changes  that are already affecting us  but also show you just how far we've  come  boris johnson has said the uk will  become the saudi arabia  of wind but it is a huge task to be as  big a producer of wind energy  as the gulf state is of oil in the last  20 years the contribution of wind power  to the uk's energy mix has risen from  around  320 megawatts in 2001 to more than 24  000 megawatts in 2021 a 73-fold increase  that sounds huge but it's still not  enough  according to the national grid wind  could become  the dominant source of energy in the uk  as we move towards net zero emissions  it can be generated onshore from  turbines anywhere on land  or offshore where the turbines are in  the water in terms of onshore wind look  at how  far behind the uk is compared to  countries including  china and the united states but offshore  the uk benefits from geography and comes  out  on top providing almost a third of the  global offshore wind power  our correspondent lisa holland reports  now from blithe in northumberland  home to the biggest wind turbine testing  facility in the world  the port of blyth used to be europe's  biggest exporter of coal  but has been transformed into a hub  supporting offshore energy  we're heading out to sea from the  northumberland coast to one of the uk's  fast growing number of wind farms  this is how the prime minister is hoping  to deliver on his bold boast  for the uk to be to win what saudi  arabia is to oil  the uk has what many countries don't an  abundance  of wind here in the north sea these  giant  turbines are generating clean renewable  energy  when we talk about reducing emissions  and tackling climate change  this is part of how that's happening  business leaders say the uk has been  slow to pick up on the wider benefits of  wind development  the vast majority of all wind turbine  components are built abroad  so whether that's germany denmark and  spain france etc  and that's from the top of the turbine  to  to sub-sea this is the biggest blade  test facility in the world  at 107 meters you get a sense with me  and the director of the facility walking  beneath it just how long  this prototype is the growth in this  technology has been  so rapid and that we're at capacity  and if we're going to keep the uk at the  cutting edge of this technology then we  need to continually invest  in research infrastructure the  government wants to produce enough wind  to power our homes by 2030 it may be on  track to do that in the uk but not to  generate  enough to export around the world in  large quantities  lisa holland sky news northumberland  well let's head over to our data  dashboard now and you can see the  breakdown of where electricity in great  britain  is coming from today at the moment  56 is coming from fossil fuels  26 from renewable sources including wind  and 18 from nuclear this month we've had  the lowest average minimum temperatures  for april since  1922. that's according to early figures  from the met office and we've seen  increased levels of fossil fuel use  because of it  and you can see a more detailed  breakdown of where that energy is coming  from  including how much is being generated by  wind power on  our website that's at skynews.com  forward slash  climate let's take a look at some of the  day's other climate news now  climate activists have blocked an  entrance to the fas lane nuclear base in  scotland  members of extinction rebellion and  trident ploughshares  attach themselves to giant plant pots at  the north gate of the base in argyll and  bute  the group say they want a future that's  safe from the threat of nuclear weapons  and environmental disasters co-op has  become the latest supermarket to say  it will stop selling plastic bags for  life the retailer said lots of people  only use the 10 pence bags once before  throwing them away  leading to a rise in plastic use it's  estimated the move will reduce plastic  waste  by 870 million tons every year  and we're being encouraged to let our  gardens go wild plant life wants people  to leave their grass unmowed  and let wild flowers bloom on their  lawns as part of no  mo may the nature charity says it will  help boost the number of daisies  dandelions and other flowers that  provide food for pollinators  in mexico the average person eats 8.6  kilograms of beans  each year but growing them takes  billions of liters  of water and in latin america up to 80  percent of bean yields can be lost  during an early drought  now scientists at the university of  sheffield in collaboration  with experts in mexico have developed a  bean which uses  40 percent less water saving up to  four and a half billion liters a year  i'm joined now by professor alejandra  covarrubias from the plant molecular  biology department  at national autonomous university of  mexico who worked alongside  the university of sheffield's institute  for sustainable  food alejandra thanks very much indeed  for being with us tell us about the new  bean and why it's important  well um this new bean is in progress and  still but it's very very important for  mexico because and  all latin america and even africa  because um  one of the main problems for the  cultivation of this crop  is the is the water you know most of the  of the bean is grown under rain  conditions  so it depends mainly on rain water  so we can have lost eat more  than 70 percent of the of this of this  crop  so it's very very important to have a  crops that are drought tolerant  and that can stand these very  uh severe conditions in the environment  so so it would be very very important to  have this  these kind of beans growing in mexico  and latin america  yeah so there's a double benefit crops  that survive  drought using less water but also  providing food security then for  millions yeah sure  this this uh mexico has a lot of  varieties of beans but because as you  may know  uh is the origin of this of this crop is  in is in mexico in west america  so it has been improved along the  around thousand years so we have many  different kind of  bees that have very different uh  nutritional  characteristics so we may say that beans  are  almost the perfect food because it has  fiber it has carbohydrates it can  it has a lot of protein and so in that  sense  it will have uh as you say uh  two main advantages we will have  beens that can stand low amount of water  in the soil and also bees that are have  a high  values of nutrition yeah how urgent  professor is the need to develop more  climate resistant crops  like these yeah it's very very urgent  the mexico  and africa uh it has a lot of arid  and semi-arid soils so  now the the drought is really  um invading most of the territory of our  country  and of course of africa and many other  territories in south america  so orton is very urgent to have this  kind of crowds  growing in the world actually  professor alejandra covarrubias great to  hear from you thank you  two technologies to make air  conditioning more climate-friendly have  won an international competition worth a  million dollars  the prototypes have five times less  climate impact than standard air  conditioning units  when scaled up such technologies could  prevent 132 gigatonnes of emissions  between now  and 2050 and mitigate over half a degree  of global warming  by the end of the century well joining  me now is dr eradica kosler associate  professor  at the smith school of enterprise and  environment at the university of  oxford reddick thanks very much indeed  for being with us in a  context of climate change how important  are innovations like these  they're incredibly important now we know  that the in terms of air conditioning  the number is that an equivalent of 10  new air conditioners  every second for the next 30 years are  going to enter the global market  so you can imagine how enormous the  energy demand that is going to come from  air conditioning is  and innovations like this and prices  like these are are necessary for us to  be able to bring a step change  in reduction in the amount of energy  that air conditioners are using  and so i think this is an excellent  initiative and i'm delighted to see the  two winners that that have emerged  we're kind of in a trap situation at the  moment aren't we the hotter the climate  gets  the more cold air we need to use and  that then contributes more  to global warming so how do we get out  of that  yes this is indeed a terrible feedback  loop particularly uh  one that haunts air conditioning because  we need air conditioning to to keep us  cooler but that's actually making us  warmer as a planet just as you said now  the ways to get around this are  uh both have have solutions both in the  active  cooling world which is the use of  technologies and in the passive cooling  world which is  when we use non-mechanical and energy or  non-electrified  energy to be able to provide our cooling  needs  so for instance um one big way is to  actually increase the energy efficiency  of air conditioners themselves and  that's that's precisely what this price  does  and other ways to focus on passive  cooling solutions so for instance  using phase change materials for  buildings for windows  increasing the insulation of the built  environment itself  using vegetation as a way to lower  temperatures  and then also to use behaviors right so  to sort of say  what is the set point temperature at  which we put our air conditioner is that  really how cool we need the room to be  or could it be a few degrees warmer and  still provide thermal comfort  do we leave the air conditioner on when  we're outside the room for instance  so using behaviors as a way to moderate  energy demand is also another way in  which to break  that that feedback loop which is which  is quite a difficult one  dr radeko kosler thank you very much  that's it for today's show but you can  get your climate fix over the weekend  with our podcast on this week's episode  we're talking fashion  anna jones is joined by top designers  luxury brands and activists to find out  how we can make sure the most glamorous  of industries doesn't cost the earth  that's on climatecast and it's available  wherever you normally get your podcasts  from  and on our weekly digital show this week  we're finding out the climate impact of  cryptocurrency  and hear the behind-the-scenes story of  how our correspondent tom cheshire  filmed at a secret bitcoin farm in china  that's available on sky news social  channels  our app and website thanks for watching  and we'll see you next week  you

